WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.630 --> 00:00:05.070
- [Rachel] Hey, Joseph,
how excited are you

00:00:05.070 --> 00:00:06.570
to answer 54 questions?

00:00:06.570 --> 00:00:07.980
- Rachel, I'm super excited.

00:00:07.980 --> 00:00:09.270
- [Rachel] Can you introduce yourself?

00:00:09.270 --> 00:00:10.860
- Yeah, so my name is Joseph Ravichandran.

00:00:10.860 --> 00:00:13.140
I'm a second-year PhD
student here at MIT-CSAIL,

00:00:13.140 --> 00:00:14.250
studying computer science.

00:00:14.250 --> 00:00:15.510
- [Rachel] And what are you working on?

00:00:15.510 --> 00:00:17.550
- So I work on computer
architectural security,

00:00:17.550 --> 00:00:20.280
which means that I hack
CPUs at the Silicon level.

00:00:20.280 --> 00:00:21.540
- [Rachel] Now, when you tell people

00:00:21.540 --> 00:00:24.390
you're a computer scientist,
what are their reactions?

00:00:24.390 --> 00:00:25.590
- Honestly, most people want me to hack

00:00:25.590 --> 00:00:27.330
their Facebook account.

00:00:27.330 --> 00:00:30.000
- [Rachel] So you did expose
a security vulnerability

00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:31.500
on Apple's M1 chip.

00:00:31.500 --> 00:00:32.670
How did you do that?

00:00:32.670 --> 00:00:34.710
- Yeah, so the way that
worked was we realized

00:00:34.710 --> 00:00:36.120
that the Apple M1 supports this thing

00:00:36.120 --> 00:00:37.770
called pointer authentication.

00:00:37.770 --> 00:00:38.820
And so what we did is we thought,

00:00:38.820 --> 00:00:40.770
if we were gonna make
this feature ourselves,

00:00:40.770 --> 00:00:41.790
what mistakes might we make?

00:00:41.790 --> 00:00:43.770
What flaws might we add to the system?

00:00:43.770 --> 00:00:46.290
And we bought one of the M1s
and tested it for those flaws,

00:00:46.290 --> 00:00:47.580
and we found that the mistake

00:00:47.580 --> 00:00:49.560
that we envisioned was present.

00:00:49.560 --> 00:00:50.790
- [Rachel] So how did you feel about

00:00:50.790 --> 00:00:52.770
all of the coverage that this received?

00:00:52.770 --> 00:00:55.530
- Yeah, so having the incredible
support of the community

00:00:55.530 --> 00:00:57.240
really encouraged us to do future research

00:00:57.240 --> 00:00:59.070
and made me just more
excited to keep working.

00:00:59.070 --> 00:01:01.620
- [Rachel] So is Pacman
your favorite video game?

00:01:01.620 --> 00:01:02.580
- It's definitely up there.

00:01:02.580 --> 00:01:03.840
It's definitely one of the best.

00:01:03.840 --> 00:01:06.180
- [Rachel] And what got
you interested in hacking?

00:01:06.180 --> 00:01:07.980
- Yeah, so I grew up
making games in Unity,

00:01:07.980 --> 00:01:10.440
and one day, I decided to
try to hack my own game.

00:01:10.440 --> 00:01:13.170
Realized that it's very easy
to make insecure software,

00:01:13.170 --> 00:01:14.760
and that kind of got me interested.

00:01:14.760 --> 00:01:16.050
- [Rachel] And so what's
your favorite type

00:01:16.050 --> 00:01:17.430
of challenge to hack?

00:01:17.430 --> 00:01:19.110
- So I work on binary exploitation,

00:01:19.110 --> 00:01:20.220
which means hacking programs

00:01:20.220 --> 00:01:22.110
at the low-level assembly language.

00:01:22.110 --> 00:01:23.970
- [Rachel] And how would
you describe ethical hacking

00:01:23.970 --> 00:01:25.620
to a non-technical person?

00:01:25.620 --> 00:01:27.630
- Yeah, so ethical hacking
means that I'm looking

00:01:27.630 --> 00:01:30.360
for vulnerabilities in software
to disclose them responsibly

00:01:30.360 --> 00:01:33.000
to the vendor, so that I get
there before the bad guy does.

00:01:33.000 --> 00:01:34.530
- [Rachel] Who are some of
your favorite researchers

00:01:34.530 --> 00:01:35.363
you've worked with?

00:01:35.363 --> 00:01:37.320
- Yeah, one of my favorite
researchers to work with here

00:01:37.320 --> 00:01:39.210
is my advisor, Menja Yen.

00:01:39.210 --> 00:01:41.372
Oh, what's going on, Menja?

00:01:41.372 --> 00:01:43.530
- How's the long summer.
Do students like it?

00:01:43.530 --> 00:01:46.410
- Oh, it's been going
great. Everyone loves it.

00:01:46.410 --> 00:01:48.510
- [Rachel] What's your favorite
hacking tool or software?

00:01:48.510 --> 00:01:50.400
- It's a tool called
Ghidra that was released

00:01:50.400 --> 00:01:52.590
by the NSA, and I use it
pretty much every day.

00:01:52.590 --> 00:01:54.540
- [Rachel] Now, what's your
favorite MIT hacker prank

00:01:54.540 --> 00:01:55.860
that's ever happened?

00:01:55.860 --> 00:01:57.300
- Honestly, gotta be the cop car,

00:01:57.300 --> 00:01:58.530
which is right up there actually.

00:01:58.530 --> 00:02:00.810
And it's a classic, it's super cool.

00:02:00.810 --> 00:02:01.643
- [Rachel] What's your process

00:02:01.643 --> 00:02:03.240
for finding vulnerabilities in a system?

00:02:03.240 --> 00:02:05.520
- Yeah, so there's really two
major approaches that I take.

00:02:05.520 --> 00:02:07.200
The first is something called fuzzing,

00:02:07.200 --> 00:02:09.420
where you kind of randomly
put inputs into a program

00:02:09.420 --> 00:02:10.830
and just kind of see what breaks.

00:02:10.830 --> 00:02:13.110
And the second is just
looking at the source code

00:02:13.110 --> 00:02:15.420
and trying to figure out
what did the author miss,

00:02:15.420 --> 00:02:16.920
what kinda mistakes might they have made?

00:02:16.920 --> 00:02:18.390
- [Rachel] How do you balance
your hacking interests

00:02:18.390 --> 00:02:21.060
with your academic responsibilities?

00:02:21.060 --> 00:02:22.560
- Yeah, so luckily as a grad student,

00:02:22.560 --> 00:02:25.290
most of my academic responsibilities
are hacking-related.

00:02:25.290 --> 00:02:27.540
So I get all the hacking
I'd ever really want.

00:02:27.540 --> 00:02:28.950
- [Rachel] What's the
most unexpected thing

00:02:28.950 --> 00:02:30.000
you've learned from hacking?

00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:31.650
- The most unexpected
thing I'd have to say

00:02:31.650 --> 00:02:33.150
is just becoming very comfortable

00:02:33.150 --> 00:02:35.070
as a beginner in new things.

00:02:35.070 --> 00:02:36.270
So with hacking, a lot of the time,

00:02:36.270 --> 00:02:37.140
you're a complete beginner,

00:02:37.140 --> 00:02:39.660
starting with some new
technology you've never used.

00:02:39.660 --> 00:02:41.550
And I found that the skill
actually transfers really well

00:02:41.550 --> 00:02:43.920
to a lot of areas besides engineering.

00:02:43.920 --> 00:02:45.360
- [Rachel] Common misconceptions?

00:02:45.360 --> 00:02:46.950
- The biggest misconception
is that hacking

00:02:46.950 --> 00:02:49.140
is an illegal act or criminal act.

00:02:49.140 --> 00:02:51.450
Actually a lot of hacking
is done for ethical reasons

00:02:51.450 --> 00:02:52.800
and actually helps keep you more secure,

00:02:52.800 --> 00:02:54.690
like the hacking that we do here at MIT.

00:02:54.690 --> 00:02:56.490
- [Rachel] So have you ever
taught anyone else to hack?

00:02:56.490 --> 00:02:58.140
And how'd that go?
- Yeah, absolutely.

00:02:58.140 --> 00:02:59.400
So if you wanna learn how to hack,

00:02:59.400 --> 00:03:00.780
check out our course that we teach

00:03:00.780 --> 00:03:03.240
that I helped design called
MIT Secure Hardware Design.

00:03:03.240 --> 00:03:05.340
So go ahead and Google that
and you'll see the course.

00:03:05.340 --> 00:03:07.260
- [Rachel] How would
you explain what you do

00:03:07.260 --> 00:03:08.460
to a five-year-old?

00:03:08.460 --> 00:03:10.320
- Hacking computers is
basically just tricking them

00:03:10.320 --> 00:03:12.300
into doing something they
weren't supposed to do.

00:03:12.300 --> 00:03:13.260
- [Rachel] Is there anything in this world

00:03:13.260 --> 00:03:14.310
that's unhackable?

00:03:14.310 --> 00:03:16.110
- So unfortunately I don't
think we're quite there yet

00:03:16.110 --> 00:03:17.580
in terms of the defense technology.

00:03:17.580 --> 00:03:19.707
We have plenty of innovation left to go.

00:03:19.707 --> 00:03:22.590
In the meantime, it is kind
of fun taking the latest

00:03:22.590 --> 00:03:24.480
defense innovations and
trying to break them.

00:03:24.480 --> 00:03:26.580
- [Rachel] What's the coolest
thing you've ever hacked?

00:03:26.580 --> 00:03:27.510
- Coolest thing I've ever hacked

00:03:27.510 --> 00:03:29.340
has gotta be the Linux kernel.

00:03:29.340 --> 00:03:31.050
- [Rachel] Any hacking
lingo you can teach us?

00:03:31.050 --> 00:03:32.370
- Yeah, so I got two words for you.

00:03:32.370 --> 00:03:35.310
The first is exploit, which
means to actually write a hack

00:03:35.310 --> 00:03:36.540
for a system that makes it do something

00:03:36.540 --> 00:03:37.800
it's not supposed to do.

00:03:37.800 --> 00:03:40.680
The second is vuln, which
is short for vulnerability.

00:03:40.680 --> 00:03:42.270
Vulnerabilities lead to exploits.

00:03:42.270 --> 00:03:43.170
That's the part of the system

00:03:43.170 --> 00:03:45.000
that's vulnerable to you hacking it.

00:03:45.000 --> 00:03:47.160
- [Rachel] Are you always
interested in computer science?

00:03:47.160 --> 00:03:48.870
- So I grew up learning
programming from YouTube

00:03:48.870 --> 00:03:51.090
just watching YouTube videos,
and actually even started

00:03:51.090 --> 00:03:53.430
my own YouTube channel to teach
programming to other people.

00:03:53.430 --> 00:03:55.290
- [Rachel] So what was your
reaction when you received

00:03:55.290 --> 00:03:56.970
the acceptance letter from MIT?

00:03:56.970 --> 00:03:57.930
- Oh, I was super thrilled.

00:03:57.930 --> 00:03:59.910
MIT's one of the best places
on earth to do research

00:03:59.910 --> 00:04:01.290
and I'm super happy to be here.

00:04:01.290 --> 00:04:02.760
- [Rachel] What was your first day like?

00:04:02.760 --> 00:04:03.870
- It was pretty overwhelming.

00:04:03.870 --> 00:04:05.520
I got to see the lab and meet my advisor

00:04:05.520 --> 00:04:06.870
and meet all my new lab mates.

00:04:06.870 --> 00:04:08.640
- [Rachel] And how would
you describe the community

00:04:08.640 --> 00:04:09.630
here at CSAIL?

00:04:09.630 --> 00:04:10.950
- Oh, it's absolutely world-class.

00:04:10.950 --> 00:04:12.597
Like if you're interested
in some obscure technology

00:04:12.597 --> 00:04:14.910
and you need an expert on that one thing,

00:04:14.910 --> 00:04:16.830
there's definitely someone
here that knows that.

00:04:16.830 --> 00:04:18.600
- [Rachel] Are you involved
in any clubs here at MIT?

00:04:18.600 --> 00:04:21.030
- I actually just joined the
sport TaeKwonDo team here,

00:04:21.030 --> 00:04:22.530
which has been a ton of fun.

00:04:22.530 --> 00:04:23.880
There's really nothing
quite like getting kicked

00:04:23.880 --> 00:04:27.330
in the stomach repeatedly
after a long day of research.

00:04:27.330 --> 00:04:28.740
- [Rachel] Any prominent
figures in the field

00:04:28.740 --> 00:04:29.670
who influenced you?

00:04:29.670 --> 00:04:31.650
- I'm a big Steve Wozniak fan.

00:04:31.650 --> 00:04:33.480
I was also really influenced by Zoz Brooks

00:04:33.480 --> 00:04:35.940
and the greater DEFCON
community as a whole

00:04:35.940 --> 00:04:37.320
as well as Palmer Luckey.

00:04:37.320 --> 00:04:39.090
As a matter of fact, I was
actually one of the very first

00:04:39.090 --> 00:04:41.370
Oculus Rift backers on Kickstarter.

00:04:41.370 --> 00:04:42.690
- [Rachel] Hey, you recently gave a talk

00:04:42.690 --> 00:04:44.130
at the DEFCON conference.

00:04:44.130 --> 00:04:45.210
What was that like?

00:04:45.210 --> 00:04:46.530
- Yeah, that was a dream come true.

00:04:46.530 --> 00:04:49.200
Like I said, I grew up on
YouTube watching DEFCON talks

00:04:49.200 --> 00:04:51.180
all the time, and getting
to be a part of it myself

00:04:51.180 --> 00:04:53.340
was absolutely super rewarding.

00:04:53.340 --> 00:04:54.173
- [Rachel] And during your talk,

00:04:54.173 --> 00:04:55.980
you mentioned memory corruption tasks.

00:04:55.980 --> 00:04:58.020
Can you briefly explain what those are?

00:04:58.020 --> 00:04:59.730
- Yeah, so memory corruption
attacks are basically

00:04:59.730 --> 00:05:00.854
just really confusing the computer

00:05:00.854 --> 00:05:02.760
into doing something that it shouldn't.

00:05:02.760 --> 00:05:03.900
- [Rachel] All right, is there an area

00:05:03.900 --> 00:05:05.610
of computer science
research you'd really like

00:05:05.610 --> 00:05:07.110
to work on but you haven't yet?

00:05:07.110 --> 00:05:08.820
- Yeah, so one thing I've
never actually touched really

00:05:08.820 --> 00:05:10.230
is programming language research

00:05:10.230 --> 00:05:12.030
or writing new programming languages.

00:05:12.030 --> 00:05:13.080
So it might be kind of cool to work

00:05:13.080 --> 00:05:15.720
on how do you build a new
programming language for security.

00:05:15.720 --> 00:05:17.160
- [Rachel] Are there some recent research

00:05:17.160 --> 00:05:18.630
you're excited about?

00:05:18.630 --> 00:05:20.430
- Yeah, so my group actually
just started pivoting more

00:05:20.430 --> 00:05:22.920
into formal verification,
which I think is pretty cool.

00:05:22.920 --> 00:05:25.200
- [Rachel] Can you tease
any future research?

00:05:25.200 --> 00:05:27.420
- I would love to, but it's top secret.

00:05:27.420 --> 00:05:29.340
- [Rachel] So if you're
stuck on a coding issue,

00:05:29.340 --> 00:05:32.190
what are your ways to brainstorm
or figure out the problem?

00:05:32.190 --> 00:05:34.080
- Yeah, so a big one for
me is hitting the gym,

00:05:34.080 --> 00:05:35.040
lifting some weights.

00:05:35.040 --> 00:05:37.890
Also going for a walk or
just a nice game of chess.

00:05:37.890 --> 00:05:38.730
- [Rachel] Are you more productive

00:05:38.730 --> 00:05:40.110
in the morning or at night?

00:05:40.110 --> 00:05:41.970
- Gotta be honest, I'm a total night owl.

00:05:41.970 --> 00:05:43.980
- [Rachel] What's one task
you wish was automated

00:05:43.980 --> 00:05:45.210
but isn't that the moment?

00:05:45.210 --> 00:05:46.860
- Ugh. Laundry.

00:05:46.860 --> 00:05:48.600
- [Rachel] What do you think
about this crazy building?

00:05:48.600 --> 00:05:50.220
- Oh, I absolutely love working here.

00:05:50.220 --> 00:05:51.570
The fact that this place is so great

00:05:51.570 --> 00:05:53.850
is precisely because it is so crazy.

00:05:53.850 --> 00:05:56.910
All the crazy turns and twists
make you constantly lost,

00:05:56.910 --> 00:05:58.230
and this means that
you're constantly bumping

00:05:58.230 --> 00:06:00.030
into people that you
might not have otherwise.

00:06:00.030 --> 00:06:01.590
And so this means that
a lot of conversations

00:06:01.590 --> 00:06:02.970
and collaborations happen

00:06:02.970 --> 00:06:04.560
that you probably wouldn't
get in a normal building.

00:06:04.560 --> 00:06:05.610
- [Rachel] What's more annoying,

00:06:05.610 --> 00:06:07.560
software bugs or hardware defects?

00:06:07.560 --> 00:06:09.090
- Definitely hardware defects.

00:06:09.090 --> 00:06:10.500
So the problem with the hardware defect

00:06:10.500 --> 00:06:12.570
is that you can't run around
putting a new transistor

00:06:12.570 --> 00:06:14.070
on every chip you've already shipped.

00:06:14.070 --> 00:06:15.990
So that makes them way worse.

00:06:15.990 --> 00:06:17.760
- [Rachel] And what's more annoying,

00:06:17.760 --> 00:06:20.310
a room full of bugs or
software filled with them?

00:06:20.310 --> 00:06:21.510
- Definitely a room full of bugs.

00:06:21.510 --> 00:06:23.160
'Cause most software
already is full of bugs.

00:06:23.160 --> 00:06:25.920
- [Rachel] Now let's do some
favorites, starting with chips.

00:06:25.920 --> 00:06:27.090
- Apple M1.

00:06:27.090 --> 00:06:28.560
- [Rachel] Favorite
computer science resource?

00:06:28.560 --> 00:06:29.430
- YouTube.

00:06:29.430 --> 00:06:31.320
- [Rachel] Favorite
computer architecture book.

00:06:31.320 --> 00:06:32.460
- Hennessy and Patterson.

00:06:32.460 --> 00:06:33.293
- [Rachel] Favorite class
you've taken so far?

00:06:33.293 --> 00:06:36.300
- Nickolai Zeldovich's
security class here at MIT.

00:06:36.300 --> 00:06:37.530
- [Rachel] Favorite place on campus?

00:06:37.530 --> 00:06:38.363
- The gym.

00:06:38.363 --> 00:06:40.110
- [Rachel] Favorite MIT traditions.

00:06:40.110 --> 00:06:42.090
- MIT Henge, where the light from one end

00:06:42.090 --> 00:06:43.590
of the infinite corridor lines up

00:06:43.590 --> 00:06:45.600
and perfectly shines
through the entire hallway.

00:06:45.600 --> 00:06:47.340
- [Rachel] Favorite music
that gets you in the zone

00:06:47.340 --> 00:06:48.420
to do research?

00:06:48.420 --> 00:06:50.490
- 90's Rock.
- [Rachel] Favorite concert?

00:06:50.490 --> 00:06:52.080
- Actually, I just saw
Blink this past weekend

00:06:52.080 --> 00:06:53.010
and they were really good.

00:06:53.010 --> 00:06:55.200
- [Rachel] Best sci-fi movie.
- Interstellar.

00:06:55.200 --> 00:06:56.820
- [Rachel] Best brand food
for a long day's work.

00:06:56.820 --> 00:06:57.653
- Steak.

00:06:57.653 --> 00:06:58.680
- [Rachel] Favorite restaurant.

00:06:58.680 --> 00:07:00.510
- El Jefe's tacos are really good.

00:07:00.510 --> 00:07:02.040
- [Rachel] Dream job after graduation?

00:07:02.040 --> 00:07:03.840
- Literally anything that
just lets me hack stuff.

00:07:03.840 --> 00:07:05.730
- [Rachel] Where do you
see computer architecture

00:07:05.730 --> 00:07:07.860
and security headed in the next decade?

00:07:07.860 --> 00:07:10.680
- I'm mostly excited to see
where we go with risk CPUs.

00:07:10.680 --> 00:07:12.300
- [Rachel] Now, what's one piece of advice

00:07:12.300 --> 00:07:14.790
from an MIT professor that's
really stuck with you?

00:07:14.790 --> 00:07:16.560
- Yeah, I'd say the most
important piece of advice

00:07:16.560 --> 00:07:18.030
that I've been given is from Menja,

00:07:18.030 --> 00:07:20.400
which is to always apply
the scientific method

00:07:20.400 --> 00:07:23.010
even in places where it
doesn't immediately make sense.

00:07:23.010 --> 00:07:24.870
- [Rachel] And Joseph,
what advice do you have

00:07:24.870 --> 00:07:26.400
to young students who are interested

00:07:26.400 --> 00:07:28.200
in computer science and STEM?

00:07:28.200 --> 00:07:30.090
- Yeah, honestly, just start hacking.

00:07:30.090 --> 00:07:31.890
Go on YouTube, look up
some programming tutorials,

00:07:31.890 --> 00:07:33.120
and just start creating something.

00:07:33.120 --> 00:07:34.860
Doesn't have to be good. Just start.

00:07:34.860 --> 00:07:36.060
- [Rachel] Thanks so much, Joseph.

00:07:36.060 --> 00:07:38.070
- Thank you so much. Now
I gotta get back to work.

00:07:38.070 --> 00:07:38.903
See ya.

